{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '125,385p'; tail -30 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '820,875p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '125,385p'; tail -30 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '820,875p'\"","aggregated_output":"   125\t`(yin/def name <lambda macro? true>)`, exactly as `def` lowers today with the\n   126\tflag added. `^{:yang/shadow-params-operand}` / `^{:yang/shadow-body-start}`\n   127\tmetadata is retired (§3.2 explains why outermost-first expansion makes the\n   128\thint unnecessary).\n   129\t\n   130\t### 2.2 The definition form\n   131\t\n   132\tA **definition** is an `:application` whose operator is\n   133\t`{:type :variable :name yin/def}` and whose operands are\n   134\t`[{:type :literal :value <sym>} <lambda macro? true>]` — the shape\n   135\t`yang.clojure/compile-def` already emits. The expander recognises this shape\n   136\tanywhere in a batch (§3.1).\n   137\t\n   138\t**Two namespaces.** The expander's store and an evaluator's store are\n   139\tdisjoint and never reconciled. Defining a macro `m` does not remove an\n   140\tearlier function `m` from an evaluator's store — it cannot; they are on\n   141\tdifferent media — but every later `(m …)` is rewritten before it reaches the\n   142\tevaluator, so the old binding is reachable only through `m` in operand\n   143\tposition. Conversely, a plain `(yin/def m <lambda>)` after a `defmacro m`\n   144\tremoves `m` from the expander's store *and* is forwarded, so the evaluator\n   145\tbinds the function. Neither direction is silent: the expander's store is\n   146\tauthoritative for what expands, the evaluator's for what runs.\n   147\t\n   148\t### 2.3 The call site\n   149\t\n   150\tAny `:application` whose operator is a `:variable` naming a macro in scope.\n   151\t(A `:macro? true` lambda in operator position is not a supported input:\n   152\tadmission rejects a macro lambda anywhere but a definition's value operand,\n   153\t§3.1.) Operands are whatever the\n   154\tfrontend compiled them to — canonical Universal AST nodes, never\n   155\tsource-language forms. A Python `defn(\"f\", [\"x\"], x)` and a Clojure\n   156\t`(defn f [x] x)` are both `:application`s; the frontend does no discovery and\n   157\tno coercion. **Contract for macro authors:** use the prelude accessors\n   158\t(`yin/name-of`) for names rather than matching on `:type`, because a name\n   159\tmay arrive as a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string.\n   160\t\n   161\t### 2.4 The root fact and the codec\n   162\t\n   163\t`ast->datoms-with-root` appends `[root :yin/root true t default-op]`;\n   164\t`index-datoms` takes the *last* `:yin/root` fact in batch order as the\n   165\troot, falls back to the existing heuristic when none is present, and fails\n   166\t`{:rule :dangling-root :entity e}` if the named entity has no `:yin/type`. An\n   167\texplicit `:root-id` option still overrides. This is a codec improvement\n   168\tindependent of macros; the expander relies on it because it emits copies\n   169\tbottom-up and must not depend on emission order for root discovery.\n   170\t\n   171\tThe codec (`yin.vm/ast->datoms`, `datoms->ast`) keeps `:yin/macro?` on\n   172\tlambdas and gains `:yin/root`. It drops `:yin/macro-expand`,\n   173\t`:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability` and the\n   174\t`:macro-expand-event` attributes: events are written by the expander through\n   175\tits own small emitter (§4.1), not through the AST codec, because they are\n   176\tnot AST.\n   177\t\n   178\t---\n   179\t\n   180\t## 3. The expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n   181\t\n   182\t### 3.1 Contract\n   183\t\n   184\t```\n   185\texpand-batch : datoms ctx → {:status :ok     :datoms out :root-eid r :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   186\t                          | {:status :error  :error {:kind ..} :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   187\texpand       : datoms ctx → out | throws                ; convenience over expand-batch\n   188\tdefinitions  : datoms → {sym lambda-ast|nil}           ; syntactic harvest, last wins; nil = removal\n   189\tdrain-errors : expander → [expander' errors]           ; §5\n   190\tinvoke       : lambda-ast operand-asts ctx → value      ; one body, §3.3\n   191\tvalid-ast?   : value opts → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\n   192\tmark-tail    : ast → ast                               ; §3.4, whole-tree recompute\n   193\tstep         : expander → expander'                    ; one forwarder step, §5\n   194\tevent-schema                                           ; §4.1\n   195\tstdlib-forms, prelude\n   196\t```\n   197\t\n   198\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\n   199\t\n   200\t```\n   201\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\n   202\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, never re-seeded upward\n   203\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\n   204\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\n   205\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   206\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   207\t```\n   208\t\n   209\t`expand-batch`:\n   210\t\n   211\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\n   212\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\n   213\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n   214\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\n   215\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is\n   216\t   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a\n   217\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\n   218\t   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker\n   219\t   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all\n   220\t   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can\n   221\t   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if\n   222\t   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\n   223\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\n   224\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`\n   225\t   carrying a **batch-level\n   226\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\n   227\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\n   228\t   carry the offending values.\n   229\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n   230\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\n   231\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\n   232\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n   233\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\n   234\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\n   235\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\n   236\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   237\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by\n   238\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a batch whose root is a definition\n   239\t   still forwards a complete program and a REPL prints the name. The eids of\n   240\t   these stand-in literals are recorded in the batch context as\n   241\t   `:replaced {eid sym}` — an explicit internal record, independent of `m`\n   242\t   (step 5 needs it). Plain `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\n   243\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   244\t5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *form* definitions that did not have the\n   245\t   `yin/def` shape in the source — `(defn m …)` above all, but also an\n   246\t   operator that rewrites into `yin/def` — and copying along changed paths\n   247\t   gives nodes fresh eids and `default-op`, so neither eid nor `m` can say\n   248\t   where a definition came from. Step 5 therefore does not ask. The store\n   249\t   for the **next** batch is recomputed as: start from the store *as it was\n   250\t   before step 2*; walk the **final tree** in the fixed traversal order; at\n   251\t   every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` occurrence, a macro lambda `v`\n   252\t   `assoc`es and anything else `dissoc`es; at every stand-in literal\n   253\t   recorded in `:replaced`, `assoc` its source macro. Last occurrence in\n   254\t   tree order wins. Position is a property of the tree, not of the node, so\n   255\t   re-parenting cannot lose it, and a definition formed by rewriting is\n   256\t   present in the tree like any other. **Ordering rule:** step 2's\n   257\t   source-only store governs the whole current batch; step 5's result\n   258\t   governs from the next batch. Consequences, stated: `(def m f)` then\n   259\t   `(defmacro m …)` keeps the macro (its stand-in is later in the tree);\n   260\t   `(defmacro m …)` then `(defn m …)` or `((choose-def) 'm f)` removes it\n   261\t   (the formed plain definition is later); a source macro definition that\n   262\t   an enclosing macro *discards* from its output is in force for the current\n   263\t   batch (step 2) and absent from the next (not in the final tree) — the\n   264\t   final tree is the program; within one batch a macro `m` and a `(defn m\n   265\t   …)` both apply to their own forms in source order, as Clojure would not.\n   266\t   The design accepts these one-batch divergences in exchange for a\n   267\t   one-pass, deterministic rule that needs no origin tracking.\n   268\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   269\t   (§3.4).\n   270\t7. **Emit.** Output is emitted bottom-up as a self-contained batch with a\n   271\t   fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries `m` per §4.1.\n   272\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   273\t   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\n   274\t   definitions, and `:generated-macro` rejecting generated ones, this cannot\n   275\t   fire; it is the guard for decision 5 and a violation is a forwarder\n   276\t   defect, thrown as `{:kind :scan-failed :eid e}`.\n   277\t\n   278\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\n   279\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\n   280\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\n   281\thost.\n   282\t\n   283\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   284\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   285\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   286\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   287\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   288\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   289\t\n   290\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   291\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   292\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   293\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   294\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   295\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   296\t\n   297\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   298\t\n   299\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   300\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   301\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   302\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `shadow` is the\n   303\tset of names bound by enclosing `:lambda :params`.\n   304\t\n   305\t```\n   306\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\n   307\t  case operator(eid)\n   308\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   309\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)     ; defensive only: unreachable after admission (§3.1) and :generated-macro\n   310\t    _            → nil\n   311\t\n   312\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\n   313\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\n   314\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\n   315\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\n   316\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\n   317\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\n   318\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   319\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\n   320\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\n   321\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\n   322\t  else if type(eid) = :application:\n   323\t      op'       = expand-node ctx operator(eid) shadow depth        ; operator FIRST\n   324\t      eid'      = if op' = operator(eid) then eid else copy eid with operator op'\n   325\t      if macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\n   326\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; became a macro call: takes the first arm with the ORIGINAL operands\n   327\t      operands' = [expand-node ctx c shadow depth | c ∈ operands(eid)]\n   328\t      return if operands' = operands(eid) then eid' else copy eid' with operands'\n   329\t  else:\n   330\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\n   331\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\n   332\t      return if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\n   333\t```\n   334\t\n   335\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\n   336\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\n   337\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n   338\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\n   339\tacyclic by construction.\n   340\t\n   341\tThe application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that\n   342\tan operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands\n   343\t*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`\n   344\tmust receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.\n   345\tExpanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the\n   346\tmacros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the\n   347\tre-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into\n   348\tthe invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated\n   349\tapplications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.\n   350\t\n   351\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   352\t\n   353\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   354\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   355\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   356\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\n   357\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\n   358\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\n   359\t  had to decide this before expansion.\n   360\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\n   361\t  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose\n   362\t  operator was rewritten into a macro name by a child expansion\n   363\t  (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the application branch\n   364\t  re-checks after expanding the operator and before touching the operands.\n   365\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\n   366\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\n   367\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\n   368\t  accumulate depth.\n   369\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\n   370\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\n   371\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is\n   372\t  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params\n   373\t  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.\n   374\t- **Generated macro lambdas are rejected in v1 — wherever they appear.** A\n   375\t  macro whose output contains a `:macro? true` lambda, as a definition value\n   376\t  *or* inline in operator position, fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason\n   377\t  :generated-macro}`; `valid-ast?` is called with `:allow-macro-lambda?\n   378\t  false` on expansion output. With admission also rejecting source macro\n   379\t  lambdas outside definition position, `macro-of`'s inline-lambda arm is\n   380\t  unreachable and kept only as a defensive check. Macro-defining macros\n   381\t  and generated inline macros are reserved together (§7); admitting the\n   382\t  second without the first would need a rule for keeping a generated macro\n   383\t  body as transformer syntax through re-expansion, which is the same\n   384\t  problem.\n   385\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before the\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1789279450960-review-macro-stream-process-r3.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 3:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 18 | Re-check happens after operands expanded; a newly discovered binder macro gets rewritten operands (astra P1-1) | §3.2 application branch: operator first, re-check, then operands; test with `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` |\n| 19 | `(defn m …)` after `(defmacro m …)` not caught — the `yin/def` shape appears only post-expansion; non-lambda redefinitions ignored (astra P2-2) | §3.1 step 2 `dissoc`es on any non-macro value; new step 5 post-harvest over the final tree, effective next batch; ordering rule stated |\n| 20 | Inline generated macro lambda both required by a test and forbidden by the validator (astra P2-3) | Prohibition kept and widened to `:generated-macro`; the test case removed; both reserved together |\n| 21 | `:errors` never drained; a stale error suppresses later evaluation; A-ok/B-error in one call skips A (astra P2-4, glm P2-1) | §5 `drain-errors` read-and-reset with `:forwarded`; §6.1 drives the evaluator on `:forwarded > 0` independently of `:errors` |\n| 22 | Admission checks only root-reachable, harvest decodes the whole batch (astra P2-5) | §3.1 step 1 checks every entity in the index |\n| 23 | Admission failures have no event path (astra P2-6) | §4.1 admission-failure event allocated from the watermark, no input values in diagnostics |\n| 24 | `run-on-stream` error shape: a value-shaped error lets un-updated callers continue silently (glm P3-2); load vs flush failure must carry different cursors (astra) | §5 prerequisite: keep the throw, carry `:session` in `ex-data`, cursor position depends on where the failure was; Phase 0 tests |\n| 25 | Admission should check the closed node vocabulary (glm P3-3) | §3.1 step 1 `:unknown-type` |\n| 26 | `closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` are protocol outcomes, not defects; `full` may be permanent (astra) | §5 table reworded; outcome and both slots preserved in the throw |\n| 27 | Decision 5 \"unbound variable\" too strong given §2.2; decisions 10/11 out of order; §3.4 exactness under the no-inline contract (astra, glm) | Decision 5 qualified; order fixed; §3.4 sentence added |\n\nRound 4 (`collab/1789279972076-review-macro-stream-process-r4.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 4; glm approved r4:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 28 | Post-harvest removes a later winning source macro because an earlier plain source `yin/def` survives in the tree (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 is a unified last-wins over source definitions at their positions and *generated* definitions (identified by `m` = event, never eid) at their call sites' positions; consequences enumerated; four acceptance cases plus re-parented variants in Phase 1 |\n| 29 | Stray source macro lambda outside a definition shape has no disposition (glm P3-1) | §3.1 step 1 `:stray-macro-lambda` at admission; step 8 named `:scan-failed` as a forwarder defect |\n| 30 | Appendix C row 5 names the retired `:generated-definition` (glm P3-2) | Row 5 annotated |\n\nRound 5 (`collab/1789280260256-review-macro-stream-process-r5.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`), on revision 5:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 31 | `m` is emission provenance, not definition origin: a re-parented generated definition carries `default-op`, and an operator rewrite forms a `yin/def` under `default-op`; both escape post-harvest (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 ranks every `yin/def` occurrence in the *final tree* in traversal order, with step 3's recorded stand-in literals (`:replaced`) representing source macro definitions; no origin tracking, no use of eid or `m`; discarded-definition consequence stated; both examples in Phase 1 |\n| 32 | `macro-of`'s inline macro-lambda arm is unreachable after admission (astra P3) | §2.3 drops the inline case as supported input; §3.2 marks the arm defensive |\n   820\tseparate `(defn m …)` batch removes it next batch; `(defn m [x] (wrap x))`\n   821\twith `wrap` a macro (the formed definition is re-parented by a descendant\n   822\texpansion) removes it next batch; `((choose-def) 'm f)` with `choose-def` →\n   823\t`yin/def` (a definition formed by operator rewrite, `default-op`) removes it\n   824\tnext batch; each of the source cases repeated with the definition node\n   825\tre-parented (fresh eid) by an unrelated expansion in the same batch; a\n   826\tsource macro definition discarded by an enclosing macro is absent next\n   827\tbatch**; a bare `:macro? true` lambda outside a definition fails admission\n   828\tas `:stray-macro-lambda`;\n   829\toutermost-first (`->`-style macro); shadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a\n   830\t`defn`'s own params without any frontend hint; `(fn [m] (m 1))` with `m` a\n   831\tmacro is left intact; a macro name in operand position is left as a\n   832\tvariable; **an operator macro returning a macro name is expanded with its\n   833\toperands unexpanded (`((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with a macro `x` in scope\n   834\tyields a lambda whose body still says `(x 1)`), and an operator-selected\n   835\tmacro that discards an operand containing a failing macro call succeeds**;\n   836\ta macro emitting a macro lambda — as a definition or inline — fails\n   837\t`:generated-macro`; depth guard with chain; per-expansion bound from node\n   838\tcount before emission; per-batch bound; step budget on an infinite body;\n   839\tparked body rejected; **cyclic operand, dangling ref, unknown node type, a\n   840\thost value in a literal, and a cycle inside a disconnected definition all\n   841\tfail at admission before any decode, each producing one admission event\n   842\tthat survives a log `full`**; invalid output including a host function inside a\n   843\tliteral and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\n   844\tbatches with overlapping tempids when ctx is threaded, and are equal on\n   845\treplay of the same ctx**; a stream effect in a body fails with the\n   846\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines **and clears a\n   847\tstale flag on marked syntax moved into an operand and into an `if` test**;\n   848\ta macro-free deeply nested batch passes; determinism (two runs, equal\n   849\tvectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root fact; a\n   850\tcorpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes; the\n   851\tforwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream`: `out=ok, log=full`\n   852\tretries only the log; `out=full` retries only the program; repeated `full`;\n   853\tno log medium; **an expansion failure advances the cursor, stages only the\n   854\tevent, and appears in `drain-errors`; a failed batch then a successful one\n   855\tin a single `run-on-stream` call yields `:forwarded 1` and one error; a\n   856\tdrained expander reports nothing on the next drain**; a forwarder-defect\n   857\tthrow does not advance the cursor.\n   858\t\n   859\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\n   860\t\n   861\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\n   862\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\n   863\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\n   864\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; a bad macro input prints\n   865\tits error and the *next* input evaluates normally (no head-of-line block);\n   866\t`repl-state` lists the store's macro names.\n   867\t\n   868\tDeferred to the semantic VM's own Phases 1–2 (`yin.vm.semantic` and\n   869\t`linearize` do not exist yet): the `:semantic` VM type runs the same session\n   870\twith equal values, and a tail-position macro in a 10⁵-iteration loop keeps\n   871\tcontinuation depth 0 — measured by inspecting `k`, since equal values alone\n   872\tdo not prove it.\n   873\t\n   874\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\n   875\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
